// pkg/web/recover.bit — #3951: a panic in a handler, or in any middleware
// registered after this one, becomes the same 500 a `fail` already produces.
//
// THE MECHANISM IS THE RUNTIME'S PANIC BOUNDARY (epic #4739), NOT A SECOND
// ONE. `runRecovering` (stdlib/runtime/runtime.bit, #4745) is what actually
// stops a panic from taking the process down; this file's whole job is to
// arm it around `next(c)` and turn a caught panic into an ordinary `fail`,
// so it reaches `failureRes`, adapter.bit's single failure-to-response site,
// exactly as though the handler itself had failed. That is the ticket's own
// constraint: build on the adapter wrapper #3876 established, do not add a
// second interception point. `mapFailure` (errors.bit) already sends an
// error that did not opt into `HttpError` to a 500 with the generic
// `serverErrorBody`, and `failureRes` already logs `err.message()` with the
// method and the path — a recovered panic needs nothing further from either.
import { runRecovering } from "std/runtime"

// `app.use(recover())`, and MUST BE THE FIRST `app.use()` on the app: a
// panic is only caught while running inside the `runRecovering` boundary
// below, which is armed around the call to `next(c)` — so a middleware
// registered so that it becomes THIS call's `next` (anything registered
// after `recover()`, per `collectMiddleware`/`buildChain`, middleware.bit)
// is inside the boundary, and anything `recover()` is itself nested inside
// is not. This is the one place registration order matters in this
// framework — #3886 made it irrelevant everywhere else.
//
// `runRecovering` takes `() => ()` (stdlib/runtime/runtime.bit), so the
// `Res!` that `next(c)` would return cannot come back through the closure's
// own return value: it is captured into `res`/`failed` instead, exactly as
// that function's own doc comment shows. Both stay unread on the panicking
// path — `msg` below is what the runtime recovered, never a local the
// discarded frame might not have written (runtime.bit's own warning).
export fn recover(): Middleware {
  return (c, next) => {
    let res: Res
    let failed: error
    let (panicked, msg) = runRecovering(() => {
      res = next(c) catch e {
        failed = e
        return
      }
    })
    if (panicked) {
      fail newError(msg)
    }
    if (failed != nil) {
      fail failed
    }
    return res
  }
}
